package cajeta.net.dns;

/**
 * The **address-family filter** a [`Dns.resolve`](Dns.cajeta) call
 * applies to a name lookup (plan item **NET-2.2**).
 *
 * A hostname can map to IPv4 addresses, IPv6 addresses, or both. This
 * enum lets a caller restrict the result set to one family — e.g. a
 * v4-only network path wants `V4_ONLY`, a v6-only listener wants
 * `V6_ONLY`, and the default connect path wants `BOTH` so the
 * sequential v4/v6 fallback (NET-2.6) has every candidate to try.
 *
 * ## Ordinal contract — the native `getaddrinfo` family filter
 *
 * The ordinals here are **deliberately the cajeta-portable enum
 * positions** (`V4_ONLY = 0`, `V6_ONLY = 1`, `BOTH = 2`), **not** the
 * native filter values the
 * [`__cajeta_net_getaddrinfo`](../../../../native/cajeta_net_getaddrinfo.c)
 * intrinsic expects. That native filter is a different little space:
 *
 *   -  `-1` = both   (`AF_UNSPEC`)
 *   -   `0` = V4-only (`AF_INET`)
 *   -   `1` = V6-only (`AF_INET6`)
 *
 * [`Dns`](Dns.cajeta) owns the one-line mapping from this enum to that
 * native filter ([`Dns.nativeFilter`](Dns.cajeta)), so the platform
 * value never leaks into application code — the same separation
 * [`AddressFamily`](../AddressFamily.cajeta) keeps between its
 * cajeta ordinals and the platform `AF_*` constants. Do **not** assume
 * the enum ordinal equals the native filter; they intentionally
 * differ (`BOTH` is enum `2` but native `-1`).
 *
 * This is distinct from [`AddressFamily`](../AddressFamily.cajeta) on
 * purpose: `AddressFamily` discriminates *what a resolved address is*
 * (`V4` / `V6`, a two-valued property of every address); `ResolveFamily`
 * expresses *what a lookup should return* (a three-valued request,
 * including "either"). Conflating them would force a phantom "both"
 * member onto the address discriminant where it is meaningless.
 */
public enum ResolveFamily {
    V4_ONLY,
    V6_ONLY,
    BOTH
}
